package ferrum:plugin@1.0.0;

// CAPABILITY. Grant name: "commands".

/// Adjust the commands this plugin's manifest declared.
///
/// **There is no registration here, and that is the point.** Command ids,
/// titles, categories and `when` predicates live in the signed manifest — the
/// actual security boundary, reviewed by the registry and shown at consent.
/// Re-declaring them at runtime would give the host two sources of truth to
/// reconcile and would manufacture a `denied` case that exists only because of
/// the duplication. The world hands out *handles to what the manifest already
/// says*; it never registers anything.
///
/// **There is also deliberately no way to invoke a host command from a guest.**
/// An `execute(command-id)` would be an authority amplifier that reduces every
/// other capability here to advisory: a plugin holding no file capability at
/// all could run the editor's own "delete file" or "trust this workspace" and
/// inherit the user's full authority through a string. If a plugin needs an
/// effect, the effect gets its own narrow capability or it does not exist.
interface commands {
  use types.{command-id};

  /// Every command this plugin's manifest declared, in manifest order.
  all: func() -> list<command>;
  get: func(id: command-id) -> option<command>;

  /// Host-implemented, so it can gain methods for the life of major version 1.
  resource command {
    id: func() -> command-id;
    /// Override the manifest's palette title for this session — a git plugin
    /// showing the current branch, say. The manifest's title is the default
    /// and the fallback.
    set-title: func(title: string);
    /// Grey it out in the palette and refuse its keybinding. The manifest's
    /// `when` predicate still governs visibility and is evaluated host-side on
    /// the input path with no guest call: the palette must never wake a plugin
    /// to decide whether to list a row.
    set-enabled: func(enabled: bool);
  }
}
